# Keep the dependency lockfiles LF on every platform so the CI sync-check (`uv export` +
# `git diff --exit-code`) can't be tripped by Windows CRLF drift (DEP-1).
uv.lock text eol=lf
requirements.lock text eol=lf
docker/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
# constraints.lock was MISSING here (`git check-attr text -- constraints.lock` -> unspecified), so under
# core.autocrlf=true it checks out CRLF — precisely the drift this stanza exists to prevent. Harmless so
# far only because git's clean filter normalizes before `git diff`, which is also why export sync must be
# verified with `git diff` and never a raw `diff` (the latter reports a phantom whole-file difference).
constraints.lock text eol=lf
# The PEP 735 CI-toolchain exports (ADR 0034 §3) — same DEP-1 diff gate, same LF requirement.
ci/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
.mefor-hooks/** text eol=lf
# Shell scripts are executed on the Linux CI runners; force LF so a contributor's
# core.autocrlf=true can never commit CRLF (which breaks `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`).
*.sh text eol=lf

# The vendored CLA Action's compiled bundle (BACKLOG #1364): its SHA256 was checked against the
# exact upstream commit and that checksum is recorded as evidence in the ledger entry. `-text`
# disables ALL line-ending conversion so the stored and checked-out bytes never drift from what
# was verified -- `eol=lf` was tried first and silently re-encoded ~1,300 bytes, which would have
# committed a file that no longer matches its own recorded checksum.
.github/actions/**/dist/** -text

# The offline password-screening corpus (BACKLOG #1433). Its SHA-256 is recorded in the generated
# block of `common_passwords.NOTICE` and recomputed by `scripts/security/build_password_corpus.py`,
# so the checked-out bytes must not depend on the platform. `-text` (not `eol=lf`) for the same
# reason as the stanza above: it disables ALL conversion, so stored and checked-out bytes are equal.
# THE ATTRIBUTE WAS MISSING BEFORE #1433 -- `git check-attr text` reported `unspecified`, so under
# `core.autocrlf=true` the file checked out CRLF while the blob held LF. An existing Windows
# checkout still holds CRLF until the file is re-checked-out (`git checkout -- <path>`); changing an
# attribute does not rewrite a working tree. The generator normalizes CRLF to LF before digesting
# so such a checkout still verifies, rather than failing with a hex mismatch that names nothing.
messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.txt -text
# Its notice carries the generated block, which the same script writes with an explicit LF newline.
# `text eol=lf` (not `-text`): nothing digests this file, so the forgiving form is right -- git's
# clean filter normalizes an older CRLF working copy, so neither the attribute landing nor a
# regeneration shows up as a whole-file diff.
messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.NOTICE text eol=lf
